function curriculumChunk1_() {
  return {"grade6":{"g6-bridging-w1-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core","number":1,"week":1,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"Data Quality and AI Outputs","summary":"Students trace the causal chain from a specific data-quality problem to the specific output mistake it causes, and separate the quality of a dataset from its size.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU"],"objectives":["Explain, with an example, how higher- or lower-quality data changes an AI output.","Predict the effect on the output when the data quality is degraded or improved.","Distinguish the quality of a dataset from the quantity of data in it."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Data Quality and AI Outputs","body":["Imagine teaching a younger cousin to recognise dates using a photo album. If half the photos are blurry and one photo is actually a stone, incorrectly labelled as a date, what will your cousin get wrong? An AI learns the same way you would teach - from the examples it is shown. Today you become a data detective who can predict an AI's mistakes just by studying its data."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can explain, using a clear example, how the quality of data changes what an AI produces.","I can predict whether an output will get better or worse when the data is made cleaner or noisier, and say why.","I can tell the difference between the amount of data and the quality of data."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"data quality","definition":"How accurate, complete, consistent, and representative a dataset is."},{"term":"label","definition":"The name attached to an example that tells the model what it is."},{"term":"noise","definition":"Irrelevant or inconsistent detail in data that confuses the pattern."},{"term":"representative","definition":"The data covers the real range of cases the model will meet."},{"term":"causal chain","definition":"A step-by-step link from a cause to its effect."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"The model learns from its examples","body":["An AI model is not filled with rules that a person typed. Instead it studies a dataset and adjusts itself to match the patterns it finds.","This means the dataset is really the model's teacher. If the teacher shows wrong examples, the model learns the wrong lesson - and it does so confidently."],"role":"explain"},{"authored":true,"type":"diagram","variant":"flow","label":"How a data problem becomes an output mistake","heading":"One flaw, traced all the way to the answer","altText":"A chain of three boxes: a data problem leads to a wrong learned pattern, which leads to a wrong output.","caption":"This is the chain you trace on your worksheet. Start at the data, not the answer.","nodes":[{"label":"A problem in the data","detail":"one stone is labelled 'date'"},{"label":"The model learns it","detail":"small round = date"},{"label":"The output is wrong","accent":true,"detail":"it calls a pebble a date"}],"edges":[{"label":"so the model learns"},{"label":"so the output will"}],"role":"diagram"},{"type":"text","heading":"Quality is not the same as quantity","body":["It is tempting to think more data always helps. But a huge dataset full of mistakes just teaches the wrong pattern more strongly.","A small, clean, well-chosen dataset can beat a giant messy one. Before trusting any output, a careful builder checks the quality of the data behind it - and never puts personal or identifying information into a tool."],"role":"explain"},{"authored":true,"type":"reference","label":"Your dataset card","heading":"What a dataset card shows you","intro":"Your teacher gives you a card describing one dataset. Read it in this order — the flaw is never announced, you have to find it.","entries":[{"term":"The examples","asks":"How many items are there, and what is each one a picture of?","test":"A small set is not automatically a bad one."},{"term":"The labels","asks":"What is each example called? Is every label correct?","test":"One wrong label teaches one wrong lesson."},{"term":"The spread","asks":"Does the set cover the real range of cases, or only some of them?","test":"A gap is as damaging as an error."},{"term":"The noise","asks":"Is anything blurry, inconsistent or irrelevant?","test":"Noise makes the pattern harder to find."}],"role":"reference"},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will study a dataset card, predict the exact mistake the AI will make, then watch the real outputs to check your detective work."],"items":["Read your dataset card and note how each item is labelled.","Decide the quality problem: error, gap, or noise.","Predict one exact output mistake and write it down before the reveal.","Compare your prediction with the revealed clean and degraded outputs.","Write one sentence linking the data cause to the output effect."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["When could a bigger dataset actually make an AI worse?","How can an output be wrong and still sound completely sure of itself?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write one thing you can now predict about an AI just by looking at its data.","Name one place in daily life where poor-quality data could lead to an unfair result."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 1 - Core - Worksheet | Data Quality and AI Outputs","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core-aq1","type":"mcq","marks":2,"prompt":"A team trains an AI to spot dates in photos. Every photo and label is correct except one: a photo of a small grey stone has been labelled \"date\". Which mistake should you predict in the AI's output?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"It will sometimes look at a small grey stone and call it a date."},{"id":"b","text":"It will refuse to answer whenever it is shown a stone."},{"id":"c","text":"It will start calling real dates stones."},{"id":"d","text":"It will be unaffected, because one wrong photo is too small a part of the dataset to matter."}],"answer":"a","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Right - the model copies the pattern in its examples, so one stone labelled \"date\" teaches it that a small grey lump can be a date, and it says so confidently.","incorrect":"Follow the chain from the data: the stone was labelled \"date\", so the model learns that shape counts as a date and applies that lesson to new stones."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core-aq2","type":"multi","marks":3,"prompt":"A team has 40 date photos that are clear and correctly labelled. They add 4,000 more photos, but many labels in that new batch are wrong. Select every statement that is a good prediction of what happens.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The dataset is now much bigger, but lower in quality."},{"id":"b","text":"The wrong pattern will be taught more strongly than before, because thousands of examples now repeat it."},{"id":"c","text":"Knowing only that the dataset grew does not tell you the outputs will be better."},{"id":"d","text":"The outputs will improve, because the AI now has far more examples to learn from."},{"id":"e","text":"The new photos cannot do any harm, because the 40 correct photos are still in the set."}],"answer":["a","b","c"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Yes - quantity and quality are different things, and a huge dataset full of mistakes just teaches the wrong pattern more strongly.","incorrect":"More data only helps if the data is good: adding thousands of wrong labels repeats the wrong lesson, and the correct 40 cannot cancel that out."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core-aq3","type":"matching","marks":4,"prompt":"Four teams trained the same AI on different versions of a date-photo dataset. Match each dataset to the output you should expect from it.","left":[{"id":"l1","text":"A few stones are labelled \"date\"; everything else is correct."},{"id":"l2","text":"Every photo is correct, but the set contains only dark brown dates - no yellow ones."},{"id":"l3","text":"Half the photos are blurry and the labels are written as \"date\", \"dat\" and \"DATE\"."},{"id":"l4","text":"Only 12 photos, but all clear, all correctly labelled, and covering every kind of date."}],"right":[{"id":"r1","text":"It confidently calls small stones dates."},{"id":"r2","text":"It handles brown dates well but gets yellow dates wrong, because it never saw one."},{"id":"r3","text":"It is unsure and inconsistent on every kind of photo, because the pattern is hard to pick out."},{"id":"r4","text":"It gives mostly correct answers, even though it learned from a small set."}],"answer":{"l1":"r1","l2":"r2","l3":"r3","l4":"r4"},"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Well matched - an error teaches a false pattern, a gap leaves a whole case unseen, noise blurs the pattern everywhere, and a small clean set can still do well.","incorrect":"Ask what each flaw teaches the model: a wrong label teaches a wrong thing, a missing kind of date cannot be learned at all, and noise makes every case harder."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core-aq4","type":"ordering","marks":3,"prompt":"Put these four steps in order to trace how one problem in the data becomes one wrong answer.","items":[{"id":"s1","text":"A photo of a stone is put into the dataset with the label \"date\"."},{"id":"s2","text":"The model studies the dataset and adjusts itself to match the patterns it finds."},{"id":"s3","text":"The model now treats small round grey objects as dates."},{"id":"s4","text":"Shown a new photo of a pebble, the model answers \"date\"."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"That is the causal chain: a problem in the data, then a pattern learned from it, then the wrong output it produces.","incorrect":"Start at the data, not the answer - the flawed example comes first, the model learns from it, and only then does the wrong output appear."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-core-aq5","type":"truefalse","marks":1,"prompt":"A team re-photographs every blurry item so it is sharp, and rewrites the labels that had been spelled three different ways. The dataset still holds exactly the same photos of exactly the same dates - none are added and none are taken away. Based on the lesson, the AI’s outputs should get better.","answer":true,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Yes - taking out noise makes the pattern easier to learn, and that is a quality improvement even though the dataset is exactly the same size.","incorrect":"The amount of data did not change, but the noise did: less blur and consistent labels make the pattern clearer, so the outputs should improve."}}]},"formative":{"targets":"Grade 5 Advanced: explains how data quality affects AI outputs","check":"Data-quality contrast task: predict, reveal, explain the output difference","successCriteria":"Explains the data-quality to output link with an example and predicts the effect of a quality change.","evidence":"Contrast task record / think-aloud."}},"g6-bridging-w1-lab":{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab","number":2,"week":1,"track":"bridging","type":"lab","title":"Data Quality Investigation (Lab)","summary":"Teams run a fair test across four versions of one dataset, record how each output shifts from the clean baseline, and rank the datasets with output evidence.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU"],"objectives":["Run a hands-on comparison of clean vs noisy data and record how the output changes.","Rank data sets by expected output quality and justify the order.","Use evidence from the comparison, rather than opinion, to explain the data-to-output link."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Data Quality Investigation (Lab)","body":["In the Core lesson you predicted one AI mistake from its data. Today you run a proper investigation: four versions of the same dataset, four sets of outputs, and one big question - which data quality problem does the most damage? By the end you will rank them and defend your order with evidence, just like a real AI auditor."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can run a fair comparison of clean and noisy data and record what changes in the output.","I can rank several datasets from best to worst expected output and justify my ranking.","I can use evidence from the comparison to explain the data-to-output link."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"fair test","definition":"A comparison where you change only one thing so you can tell what caused a result."},{"term":"baseline","definition":"The clean result you compare all other versions against."},{"term":"evidence","definition":"A specific observation that supports a claim."},{"term":"ranking","definition":"Ordering items from best to worst using a clear reason."},{"term":"audit","definition":"A careful check of data or a system before you trust it."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Make the comparison fair","body":["To find out how data quality changes an output, you must be fair: keep the task and the model the same, and change only the dataset.","Then any difference you see in the output can be traced back to the data - that is what makes your evidence trustworthy."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Rank with evidence","body":["After you see how each dataset changed the output, you rank them from best to worst expected quality.","The hard part is the middle: when two datasets are both weak, you decide which flaw hurt the output more, and you point to the exact output change that proves it."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Your team compares four output sets against a clean baseline, records what changed, and ranks the datasets from best to worst with evidence."],"items":["Study the clean baseline output so you know what correct looks like.","For each degraded version, write exactly how the output changed.","Match each change to its data flaw.","Score each dataset on accuracy, completeness, and consistency.","Place all four on the ranking line and justify the order."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which data flaw did the most damage, and what evidence proves it?","When two datasets are both weak, how did your team decide which was worse?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one output change that most convinced you of your top ranking.","Describe how you would audit a dataset before trusting an AI built on it."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 1 - Lab - Worksheet | Data Quality Investigation (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-aq1","type":"ordering","marks":4,"prompt":"A team ran the same task with the same model on four versions of one dataset, and scored each against the clean baseline.\n\nVersion A (clean set): 19 of 20 correct.\nVersion B (blurry photos, labels spelled three ways): 14 of 20 correct, and many answers were unsure.\nVersion C (no yellow dates in the set): 11 of 20 correct - every yellow date wrong, the rest right.\nVersion D (some stones labelled \"date\"): 7 of 20 correct, and it repeated wrong labels confidently.\n\nRank the four versions by how many of the 20 items each one got right, best first.","items":[{"id":"v-b","text":"Version B - blurry photos and labels spelled three ways"},{"id":"v-d","text":"Version D - some stones labelled \"date\""},{"id":"v-a","text":"Version A - the clean set"},{"id":"v-c","text":"Version C - no yellow dates in the set"}],"answer":["v-a","v-b","v-c","v-d"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"That is a ranking built on evidence: the order follows what the outputs actually did, from 19 of 20 correct down to 7 of 20 with confident wrong labels.","incorrect":"Rank by what each version's output did, not by how bad the flaw sounds - match each version back to its recorded score before placing it on the line."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-aq2","type":"mcq","marks":2,"prompt":"The team wants their investigation to show that the differences in the outputs really came from the data. Which plan makes it a fair test?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Keep the task and the model the same for every version, and change only the dataset."},{"id":"b","text":"Give each dataset a different model, so every dataset gets its best chance."},{"id":"c","text":"Change the dataset and make the task harder at the same time, so the differences are easier to see."},{"id":"d","text":"Test each dataset on a different task, then compare the average scores."}],"answer":"a","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Exactly - when only the dataset changes, any difference in the output can be traced back to the data, and that is what makes your evidence trustworthy.","incorrect":"If more than one thing changes between versions, you can no longer tell whether the data or the other change caused the difference."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-aq3","type":"multi","marks":3,"prompt":"A ranking has to be justified with evidence from the outputs, not opinion. Select every line below that counts as evidence.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The error version called 3 of the 5 stones \"date\"; the clean version called none of them dates."},{"id":"b","text":"The noisy version got 14 of 20 right - six fewer than the clean baseline."},{"id":"c","text":"The gap version got every yellow date wrong, while the baseline got all of them right."},{"id":"d","text":"Our team all agreed that the noisy dataset felt like the worst one."},{"id":"e","text":"The error version must be worst, because wrong data is always the worst thing."}],"answer":["a","b","c"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Yes - evidence is a specific observation of what the output did, and each of those points to an exact change you could show someone.","incorrect":"Agreeing as a team or stating a general rule is not evidence; evidence names the exact output change, such as how many items shifted from right to wrong."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-aq4","type":"matching","marks":4,"prompt":"Match each change the team saw in the output to the data flaw responsible for it.","left":[{"id":"l1","text":"It gives a wrong name to certain items, and repeats the same wrong name confidently every time."},{"id":"l2","text":"It gets every example of one whole category wrong, but handles the other categories well."},{"id":"l3","text":"Its answers are unsure and inconsistent across every kind of item, with no one category failing more than the rest."},{"id":"l4","text":"Its answers stay close to the baseline on every kind of item."}],"right":[{"id":"r1","text":"Wrong labels in the training data (an error)"},{"id":"r2","text":"A missing category in the training data (a gap)"},{"id":"r3","text":"Blurry photos and labels spelled three different ways (noise)"},{"id":"r4","text":"No flaw - this is the clean dataset"}],"answer":{"l1":"r1","l2":"r2","l3":"r3","l4":"r4"},"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Well matched - each flaw leaves its own fingerprint: an error is confident and repeated, a gap hits one category only, and noise spreads uncertainty everywhere.","incorrect":"Look at where the damage lands: one category only points to a gap, confident repeated wrong names point to wrong labels, and uncertainty across everything points to noise."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-aq5","type":"fillBlank","marks":2,"prompt":"Complete this line from the team's investigation log: \"We kept the task and the model the same and changed only the (1), and we compared every version against the clean result, which we call the (2).\"","blanks":["(1) the one thing they changed between versions","(2) the name for the clean result everything is compared against"],"answer":[["dataset","data set","the dataset","data","the data","datasets"],["baseline","the baseline","base line","clean baseline","the clean baseline"]],"acceptedAnswers":[["dataset","data set","the dataset","data","the data","datasets"],["baseline","the baseline","base line","clean baseline","the clean baseline"]],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"That is the whole method in one line: change only the dataset, and measure every version against the baseline so your evidence means something.","incorrect":"Only one thing changes in a fair test - the dataset - and the clean result you compare everything against is the baseline."}}]},"formative":{"targets":"Grade 5 Advanced: explains how data quality affects AI outputs","check":"Four-way contrast plus ranking task with written justification","successCriteria":"Ranks datasets by expected output quality and justifies the order using evidence from the output changes.","evidence":"Investigation log and ranking line with justification."}},"g6-bridging-w2-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core","number":3,"week":2,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"Refining Prompts for Better Outputs","summary":"Students learn a refinement loop - set the goal, judge the output, name one weakness, make a single targeted change, and re-judge - so they can say exactly why a prompt change improved the result.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["SD"],"objectives":["Improve a weak prompt so the output better meets the goal.","Explain which change improved the output and why it worked."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["You ask an AI to 'write about Al Ain' and get a dull, general paragraph. Was the AI wrong - or was the prompt?","Today you become a prompt engineer who sets a clear goal, judges the output against it, and makes one smart change at a time. Best of all, you will be able to say exactly why your change worked."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can turn a weak prompt into a stronger one that better meets the goal.","I can name the exact change I made and explain why it improved the output.","I can judge an output against the goal instead of just accepting it."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"prompt","definition":"The instruction and context you give an AI to guide its output."},{"term":"goal","definition":"What you want the output to achieve, stated clearly."},{"term":"constraint","definition":"A limit you set, such as length, tone, or format."},{"term":"ambiguous","definition":"Unclear or open to more than one meaning."},{"term":"refinement loop","definition":"Goal, judge, name the weakness, change, re-judge - repeated."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Judge before you change","body":["You cannot improve an output until you judge it honestly against the [[goal]]. Ask: what is missing, and what is off-target?","Common weaknesses in a prompt are a missing audience, missing format, missing [[constraint]], or an [[ambiguous]] task. Naming the weakness tells you exactly what to fix."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Change one thing, and know why","body":["Strong refiners change **one thing at a time** and predict why it should help before they test it. That way, when the output improves, they know which change did it.","Specificity comes from the task, the audience, and the format - never from personal or private information. A precise [[prompt]] is not a personal one."],"role":"explain"},{"authored":true,"type":"diagram","variant":"loop","label":"Refining is a loop, not a line","heading":"Judge, change one thing, judge again","altText":"A loop: judge the output you got, change one thing about the prompt, run it again, then judge whether that change helped.","steps":[{"label":"Judge what you got","detail":"against what you wanted"},{"label":"Change one thing","detail":"and write down which"},{"label":"Run it again","detail":""},{"label":"Judge again","detail":"better, worse, or no change?","accent":true}],"repeatLabel":"not good enough yet — go round again","caption":"A prompt is rarely right first time, and that is normal. **Keeping the receipts — which change, what happened — is what turns guessing into refining.**","role":"diagram"},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will take a weak prompt with a clear goal, name one weakness, write a targeted v2, predict its effect, and check against the revealed output."],"items":["Read the weak prompt and its goal.","Judge the v1 output and name one weakness.","Write a v2 that targets only that weakness.","Predict how the output should change and why.","Compare with the revealed v2 output and log it."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Why is changing one thing at a time smarter than changing many?","How can you make a prompt specific without adding any private information?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one change that most improved your output and why it worked.","Describe a time a clearer instruction would have helped you get a better result in real life."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 2 - Core - Worksheet | Refining Prompts for Better Outputs","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-aq1","type":"mcq","marks":2,"prompt":"Goal: persuade Grade 6 students to join the chess club. The prompt was: \"Write 4 sentences about the chess club for Grade 6 students.\" The output was 4 clear sentences saying when and where the club meets, but it did not try to persuade anyone to join. Judging the output against the goal, which weakness should you name before writing v2?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The prompt never says what the writing should do, so the AI just described the club instead of persuading."},{"id":"b","text":"The prompt never says who will read it, so the AI wrote for the wrong audience."},{"id":"c","text":"The prompt never sets a length, so the AI did not know how much to write."},{"id":"d","text":"The prompt contains too many words, so the AI got confused about the topic."}],"answer":"a","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Exactly - the audience and the length were already in the prompt, so the missing piece was the task itself: 'persuade' was never asked for.","incorrect":"Re-read the prompt: it already names Grade 6 students and asks for 4 sentences, so the gap is the task - it never tells the AI to persuade."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-aq2","type":"ordering","marks":4,"prompt":"Sara wants a better notice from her prompt. Put her actions in the order of the refinement loop taught in this lesson.","items":[{"id":"i1","text":"She writes v2, changing only the one weakness she named."},{"id":"i2","text":"She decides what the notice must achieve and writes that goal down."},{"id":"i3","text":"She judges the v2 output against the same goal to see if it improved."},{"id":"i4","text":"She reads the v1 output and compares it with her goal."},{"id":"i5","text":"She names the one weakness that best explains the gap."}],"answer":["i2","i4","i5","i1","i3"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"That is the loop: set the goal, judge, name the weakness, make one change, then re-judge against the same goal.","incorrect":"The goal comes first because you cannot judge an output without one, and the weakness must be named before you know what to change."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-aq3","type":"multi","marks":3,"prompt":"Goal: a science-fair notice that Grade 3 students can read on their own. The prompt was \"Write a notice about the science fair.\" The output was correct but used long words like 'methodology' and 'apparatus'. Which changes to the prompt would target that weakness? Choose all that apply.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Add: \"Write it for Grade 3 students.\""},{"id":"b","text":"Add: \"Use short sentences and words an 8-year-old knows.\""},{"id":"c","text":"Add: \"Include the date, time and room of the fair.\""},{"id":"d","text":"Add: \"Make it 200 words instead of 100.\""},{"id":"e","text":"Add: \"Use a formal tone suitable for the school principal.\""}],"answer":["a","b"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Both changes tell the AI who is reading and how to write for them, which is exactly the weakness you named.","incorrect":"The weakness was the reading level, so only changes about the audience or the words used will fix it - adding details, more length or a formal tone does not."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-aq4","type":"truefalse","marks":1,"prompt":"A student changes the audience, the length and the format of a prompt all at once, and the new output is much better. She can be sure which of those three changes made it better.","answer":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Right - with three changes at once the improvement could come from any of them, which is why refiners change one thing at a time.","incorrect":"A better output only proves that something helped; changing one thing at a time is what lets you say which change caused it."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-aq5","type":"matching","marks":4,"prompt":"Each output below missed the goal in a different way. Match the problem with the single change to the prompt that targets it.","left":[{"id":"L1","text":"The notice used words the Grade 3 class could not read."},{"id":"L2","text":"The answer filled two pages when a short notice was wanted."},{"id":"L3","text":"The answer came back as one long paragraph when a list was needed."},{"id":"L4","text":"The answer was about the wrong school event entirely."}],"right":[{"id":"R1","text":"State the exact event the writing is about."},{"id":"R2","text":"Name who will be reading it."},{"id":"R3","text":"Ask for the output as a bulleted list."},{"id":"R4","text":"Set a limit, such as four sentences."}],"answer":{"L1":"R2","L2":"R4","L3":"R3","L4":"R1"},"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Each fix targets one named weakness - audience, constraint, format or an ambiguous task - which is why you can tell what caused the improvement.","incorrect":"Name the weakness first, then pick the change that speaks to it: a reading-level problem needs an audience, and a too-long answer needs a limit."}}]},"formative":{"targets":"Grade 5 Advanced: refines prompts to improve output quality and explains why","check":"Prompt-refinement log: v1 to v2 with a reasoned justification","successCriteria":"Makes a refinement that measurably improves the output and explains the causal reason.","evidence":"Before/after prompt log."}},"g6-bridging-w2-lab":{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab","number":4,"week":2,"track":"bridging","type":"lab","title":"Prompt Refinement Workshop (Lab)","summary":"Teams iterate a weak prompt across several versions with a one-change-per-version log, then refine for clarity and accuracy together and justify a best-balanced version from their trade-off evidence.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["SD"],"objectives":["Iterate a prompt across several versions to raise output quality, logging each change and its effect.","Refine for two goals at once (clarity and accuracy) and discuss the trade-off."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["In the Core lesson you made one smart change to a prompt. Today you run a full workshop: version 1, 2, 3, maybe 4 - each a single change you can defend.","Then comes the real challenge of a designer: chasing two goals at once, clarity and accuracy, and discovering that helping one can cost the other. Your log will prove exactly what worked."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can iterate a prompt across several versions and log each change and its effect.","I can refine for two goals at once and explain the trade-off between them.","I can use my log as evidence for which change helped the most."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"iterate","definition":"To improve something step by step across several versions."},{"term":"trade-off","definition":"When improving one goal makes another goal worse."},{"term":"balance","definition":"The best compromise between competing goals for a purpose."},{"term":"accuracy","definition":"How correct and on-topic the output is."},{"term":"clarity","definition":"How easy the output is to understand."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"Iterate and keep the receipts","body":["A designer does not stop at the first improvement. You [[iterate]] - version by version - changing one thing each time and logging its effect.","The log is your **evidence**. When someone asks which change helped most, you point to the row that proves it, because you changed only one thing that version."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"text","heading":"Two goals, one trade-off","body":["Now aim for two goals at once: [[clarity]] and [[accuracy]]. Sometimes a change that makes the output clearer quietly drops an accurate detail.","That is a [[trade-off]]. Your job is not to pretend it away but to choose the version that best balances both goals for your purpose - and to say why."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Your team iterates a weak prompt across several versions, logging each change and effect, then balances clarity and accuracy and chooses the best version with evidence."],"items":["Set the goal and judge the v1 output.","Make v2 with one change; log the reason and revealed effect.","Iterate to v3 and v4, one change each, logging cause and effect.","Add accuracy as a second goal alongside clarity.","Find and record a trade-off where one goal cost the other.","Choose the best-balanced version and justify it from your log."],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which single change gave the biggest quality jump, and how does your log prove it?","Where did clarity and accuracy pull against each other, and how did you decide?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the best-balanced version your team chose and one sentence on why.","Explain what balancing two goals taught you about making design decisions."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 2 - Lab - Worksheet | Prompt Refinement Workshop (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-aq1","type":"truefalse","marks":1,"prompt":"A team iterates a prompt from v1 to v4. Because v4 is the newest version, it must be the team's best-balanced version.","answer":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Right - a later change can cost you on one goal, so the best balance is the version your log shows fits the purpose, not simply the last one.","incorrect":"Each version changes one thing, and a change can help clarity while hurting accuracy - so v4 is best only if the log shows it balances both goals for your purpose."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-aq2","type":"matching","marks":4,"prompt":"A team iterated a notice about the school recycling drive, one change per version, and scored each output out of 5 for clarity and accuracy.\nv1 \"Write about the recycling drive.\" - clarity 2, accuracy 3\nv2 added \"write it in 4 sentences\" - clarity 4, accuracy 3\nv3 added \"for Grade 3 readers\" - clarity 5, accuracy 1 (the collection dates disappeared)\nv4 added \"include the collection dates\" - clarity 4, accuracy 4\nMatch each version with what its log entry shows.","left":[{"id":"L1","text":"v2: added \"write it in 4 sentences\""},{"id":"L2","text":"v3: added \"for Grade 3 readers\""},{"id":"L3","text":"v4: added \"include the collection dates\""},{"id":"L4","text":"v1: \"Write about the recycling drive.\""}],"right":[{"id":"R1","text":"Brought a true detail back, costing a little clarity."},{"id":"R2","text":"Made the notice clearer and left accuracy exactly as it was."},{"id":"R3","text":"The starting point, with nothing yet to compare it against."},{"id":"R4","text":"Gave the clearest output of all, but a true detail was lost."}],"answer":{"L1":"R2","L2":"R4","L3":"R1","L4":"R3"},"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Because only one thing changed each time, every row of the log can be read as the cause of that version's effect.","incorrect":"Compare each version's two scores with the version before it: that difference is the effect of the one change made in that version."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-aq3","type":"numeric","marks":2,"prompt":"Using the same log (v1 clarity 2 / accuracy 3; v2 clarity 4 / accuracy 3; v3 clarity 5 / accuracy 1; v4 clarity 4 / accuracy 4), how many accuracy points did the team give up when they moved from v2 to v3?","answer":2,"tolerance":0,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Accuracy fell from 3 to 1, so the clearer v3 cost 2 accuracy points - that number is the size of the trade-off.","incorrect":"Subtract v3's accuracy score from v2's: 3 - 1 = 2. Measuring the loss is how you show a trade-off with evidence instead of just claiming one."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-aq4","type":"multi","marks":3,"prompt":"Using the same log (v1 clarity 2 / accuracy 3; v2 clarity 4 / accuracy 3; v3 clarity 5 / accuracy 1, dates dropped; v4 clarity 4 / accuracy 4), which statements does the log actually support? Choose all that apply.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"v2's change raised clarity without changing accuracy."},{"id":"b","text":"v3's change raised clarity but lowered accuracy."},{"id":"c","text":"v4 is the only version scoring 4 or more on both goals."},{"id":"d","text":"Every version after v1 improved both goals."},{"id":"e","text":"v3 lost accuracy because the team changed two things in that version."}],"answer":["a","b","c"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"You read the log as evidence: two of the changes helped one goal only, and just one version reaches 4 on both.","incorrect":"Check each claim against the numbers - v3's accuracy fell, so not every version improved both goals, and the log records only one change per version."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-aq5","type":"mcq","marks":2,"prompt":"The notice will be handed to Grade 3 students and it must tell them the correct collection dates. Using the same log (v1 clarity 2 / accuracy 3; v2 clarity 4 / accuracy 3; v3 clarity 5 / accuracy 1, dates dropped; v4 clarity 4 / accuracy 4), which choice of best-balanced version is best justified by the evidence?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"v4, because it keeps clarity high at 4 while bringing the correct dates back."},{"id":"b","text":"v3, because it scored highest of all the versions on clarity."},{"id":"c","text":"v2, because it was the first version that improved on v1."},{"id":"d","text":"v1, because it was written before any goal pulled against another."}],"answer":"a","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"The purpose needs both goals, and v4 is the only version the log shows meeting both - a clear notice that still carries the right dates.","incorrect":"Judge against the purpose: a notice missing the collection dates fails its job however clear it reads, so the highest clarity score alone cannot justify the choice."}}]},"formative":{"targets":"Grade 5 Advanced: refines prompts to improve output quality and explains why","check":"Iteration log across several versions plus a two-goal trade-off justification","successCriteria":"Iterates a prompt with a reasoned log and chooses a best-balanced version, explaining the trade-off with evidence.","evidence":"Multi-version refinement log with trade-off note."}},"g6-bridging-w3-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core","number":5,"week":3,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"How Data Shapes a Solution's Outcome","summary":"Students trace how the examples a fruit-ripeness sorter learned from decide the answer it gives, then predict how changing that data would move the outcome.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CE"],"objectives":["Explain how the data used changes the outcome of a given solution.","Predict how a change in the data would change the solution's result."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Two apps can share the exact same code and still disagree about the same photo. The secret is not in the code it is in what each app was shown. Today you become a data detective, tracing how the examples an app learns from decide the answer it gives."],"role":"intro"},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can explain, in my own words, how the data fed into a solution shapes the outcome it produces.","I can predict what would happen to the outcome if the data were changed, and give a reason.","I can point to a specific part of the data that most affects the result."],"role":"objectives"},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"solution","definition":"The whole system that solves a problem - the data plus the model built from it."},{"term":"data","definition":"The examples a solution learns from."},{"term":"outcome","definition":"The answer or result the solution gives."},{"term":"pattern","definition":"The regularity a model finds across many examples."},{"term":"balance","definition":"Whether the data has roughly fair amounts of each category."}],"role":"vocabulary"},{"type":"text","heading":"A solution is data plus a model","body":["When people say an app 'decides', they often picture a programmer writing every rule by hand. Machine-learning solutions do not work that way. They build a **model** by studying [[data]] - lots of examples - and capturing the [[pattern]]s inside.","Because the pattern comes from the examples, the examples quietly control the answer. Feed the model different data and it learns a different pattern, so it gives a different [[outcome]], even with the very same code."],"role":"explain"},{"authored":true,"type":"diagram","variant":"parts","label":"What a solution is made of","heading":"Data plus a model","whole":"An AI solution","altText":"A bar split into two parts — the data and the model — which together produce the outcome.","parts":[{"label":"The data","detail":"the examples it learned from","accent":true},{"label":"The model","detail":"the pattern it found in them"}],"result":"The outcome you see","caption":"Change the data and the outcome changes, even with the same model. **That is why tracing a bad outcome starts at the data**, not at the answer.","role":"diagram"},{"type":"text","heading":"Amount and balance both matter","body":["Data influences an outcome in two ways. First, the *amount*: if the model saw 100 sunny photos and only 3 rainy ones, it treats sunny as normal and stumbles on rain.","Second, the [[balance]]: adding more of what the model already has plenty of changes little, but adding examples of a rare or missing category can move the outcome a lot. A data detective looks for the gap."],"role":"explain"},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Your teacher will run a fruit-ripeness sorter on the board. Your job is not to run it - it is to trace why it answers the way it does, using the data list on your card."],"items":["Read the data list on your card and say what the sorter mostly saw.","Watch one fruit go through and note the outcome.","Draw an arrow from the data batch you think caused that outcome.","Write: 'The outcome is ___ because the data mostly showed ___.'","Check your reason against a second fruit - does the story still hold?"],"role":"activity"},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which data batch had the biggest effect on the outcome, and how do you know?","If you could add only one batch of new data, which would you add to make the sorter fairer?"],"role":"discuss"},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write one sentence explaining how data shaped today's outcome.","Note one thing you would check before trusting a solution's answer."],"role":"reflect"}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 3 - Core - Worksheet | How Data Shapes a Solution's Outcome","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-aq1","type":"mcq","marks":2,"prompt":"Two date-sorting apps run exactly the same code. Shown the same photo of a brown date, one app answers 'ripe' and the other answers 'unripe'. What is the best explanation?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"The two apps learned from different sets of example photos, so each one found a different pattern."},{"id":"b","text":"One app's code must contain a mistake, because identical code always has to give identical answers."},{"id":"c","text":"One app is guessing, because apps answer at random whenever a photo is hard to judge."},{"id":"d","text":"The apps were given the same examples, but one of them studied the photo for longer."}],"answer":"a","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Right - the code is only half of a solution; the examples it learned from decide the pattern, and the pattern decides the outcome.","incorrect":"Identical code can still disagree: each app built its model from different examples, so each learned a different pattern and gives a different outcome."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-aq2","type":"ordering","marks":3,"prompt":"Put these steps in order to show how the data ends up shaping the sorter's answer about a new date.","items":[{"id":"i1","text":"The model captures the pattern it keeps seeing across those examples."},{"id":"i2","text":"Photos of dates, each labelled ripe or unripe, are collected as the data."},{"id":"i3","text":"The sorter gives an outcome for a new date it has never seen."},{"id":"i4","text":"The model studies the collected examples."}],"answer":["i2","i4","i1","i3"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Exactly - the data comes first, the pattern comes from the data, and the outcome comes from the pattern.","incorrect":"The chain runs data first: examples are collected, the model studies them, it captures a pattern, and only then can it give an outcome for a new date."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-aq3","type":"multi","marks":3,"prompt":"A sorter learned from 200 photos of brown ripe dates and only 5 photos of green unripe dates. Select ALL the changes that would move its outcomes a lot.","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Adding 60 photos of green unripe dates."},{"id":"b","text":"Adding 60 more photos of brown ripe dates."},{"id":"c","text":"Adding 60 photos of half-ripe yellow dates, a colour it has never been shown."},{"id":"d","text":"Removing 3 of the 200 brown ripe photos."},{"id":"e","text":"Running the same 200 brown ripe photos through the model a second time."}],"answer":["a","c"],"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Yes - the green and yellow batches fill gaps in categories the sorter barely had, and gaps are where the outcome moves most.","incorrect":"Adding, removing or repeating brown photos only reinforces a pattern the sorter already has; it is the missing or rare categories, green and yellow, that shift the outcome."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-aq4","type":"truefalse","marks":1,"prompt":"True or false: for a sorter that already learned from 200 brown ripe dates, adding 100 more brown ripe photos will change its outcomes more than adding 20 green unripe photos would.","answer":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Correct - size alone does not decide influence; 20 photos of a category the sorter is missing shift the outcome more than 100 more of what it already has.","incorrect":"It is false: the 100 extra brown photos repeat a pattern the sorter already learned, while the 20 green photos fill a gap, and filling a gap moves the outcome far more."}},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-aq5","type":"matching","marks":3,"prompt":"The sorter learned from 200 brown ripe dates and 5 green unripe dates. Match each change to the outcome you should predict from it.","left":[{"id":"L1","text":"Add 50 photos of green dates labelled 'unripe'."},{"id":"L2","text":"Add 50 more photos of brown dates labelled 'ripe'."},{"id":"L3","text":"Change nothing in the data, but let a different class use the app."}],"right":[{"id":"R1","text":"The sorter starts calling green dates 'unripe' - a big shift, because a missing category has been filled."},{"id":"R2","text":"The outcome barely moves, because the new examples repeat a pattern the sorter already had plenty of."},{"id":"R3","text":"The outcome stays exactly the same, because it comes from the data and the model, not from who is using the app."}],"answer":{"L1":"R1","L2":"R2","L3":"R3"},"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","feedback":{"correct":"Well matched - you predicted each outcome from what the data gained, which is exactly how a data detective reasons.","incorrect":"Predict from the gap: new examples of a missing category move the outcome, more of the same barely does, and changing the user changes nothing at all."}}]},"formative":{"targets":"Grade 4 Advanced: explains how data influences the outcome of a solution","check":"Data-to-outcome explanation on a worked solution (with a 'what if the data changed' probe)","successCriteria":"Correctly explains the data's influence and predicts the effect of a data change.","evidence":"Explanation slip + prediction."}}}};
}

